etend to care about spreadsheets or deadlines, Jimmy decided to skip work entirely. Instead, he let the open road guide him, opting for a leisurely cruise through the neighborhood. At a stoplight, he spotted a woman in a red hatchback, and the sight of her calm demeanor contrasted so oddly with his own manic laughter that he found himself cackling out loud. She, in turn, seemed to notice his bloodshot eyes and the unmistakable silhouette of a lit joint in the passenger seat. Her expression shifted from mild curiosity to alarm as she quickly rolled up her window in an attempt to escape the scene. This only made Jimmy laugh even harder, the absurdity of the moment too much to contain.\n\nAfter hours of aimless driving, with no particular destination in mind, Jimmy found himself rolling into a park. He parked his car and, without hesitation, climbed out and lay down under a large tree, letting the shade and the soft grass cradle him as he gazed up at the sky. The clouds, which to most people were just clouds, appeared to him as swirling, trippy shapes that danced and morphed into all sorts of fantastical forms. It was the perfect moment of stoner bliss—until his stomach decided to interrupt the peace with a loud, insistent growl.\n\nHungry and still in the mood for absurdity, Jimmy got back in the car and made his way to a nearby drive-thru. He had a vision of a feast in his mind and, without overthinking, ordered five burgers, two large orders of fries, and three milkshakes, just to be thorough. As he waited for his food, the intercom crackled with static, and the distorted voice that came through sounded like something from another planet. The confusion was too much for him—he rolled down the window just to see the cashier, a young employee clearly caught off guard by Jimmy’s wild eyes and giggling demeanor. He couldn’t help but laugh at the poor kid’s bewildered face, offering a wave and a nod as he took his food and left, still chuckling to himself as he drifted in